 instrumentation on the ancient Persian instruments barbat (short-necked lute), tar (long-necked lute), kamancheh (spike fiddle) and daf (frame drum).", "reference": "2008 May 30, The New York Times, “Pop and Rock Listings”, in The New York Times, archived from the original on 09 Apr 2022:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "A means or agency for achieving an aspect.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "“There the cause of death was soon ascertained ; the victim of this daring outrage had been stabbed to death from ear to ear with a long, sharp instrument, in shape like an antique stiletto, which […] was subsequently found under the cushions of the hansom. […]”", "reference": "1904–1905, Baroness Orczy [i.e., Emma Orczy], “The Tremarn Case”, in The Case of Miss Elliott, London: T[homas] Fisher Unwin, published 1905, →OCLC; republished as popular edition, London: Greening & Co., 1909, OCLC 11192831, quoted in The Case of Miss Elliott (ebook no. 2000141h.html), Australia: Project Gutenberg of Australia, February 2020:"}, {"text": "On the rocky island of Tungyin, 50 miles off the coast of Red China, is the headquarters of a little-known military unit called the Anti-Communist Salvation Army. The secret army, 30,000 strong, is Chiang Kai-shek's instrument for the long-promised return to the mainland.", "reference": "1963 January 11, “The World”, in Time, volume LXXXI, number 2, →ISSN, →OCLC, page 32, column 3:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "A measuring or displaying device.", "tags": [], "examples": ["The instrument detected an increase in radioactivity."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "A tool, implement used for manipulation or measurement.", "tags": [], "examples": ["The dentist set down his tray of instruments.", "The scientist recorded the temperature with a thermometer, b